// Ogol at a desktop terminal — the console the language is meant to be met through, on a machine
// where it can be tried without a board in the way.
//
// **It is deliberately thin, and everything interesting is in the package.** Reading a line, deciding
// whether the line finished, and printing what came back is the whole of a console's job.
//
// **The board's console is now this file with its first six lines swapped**, and that is the point of
// the shape below. `session` takes a `*Iterate[string]` and does not care what is producing the
// lines: at a terminal in cbreak mode that is a `sysl.term.edit.Editor`, over a serial cable it is
// the same `Editor` over the port, and on a pipe it is `sysl.io`'s cooked line cursor. The language
// is developed here, where a test suite runs in fifty milliseconds, and shipped to a board that
// cannot run one.
//
// The one thing this shows that the tests cannot is the **continuation prompt**, which is the reason
// `Fault` has two sides. A line that stops inside a `[` or before an `end` has not gone wrong, so it
// is held and the next line is added to it; anything else is a refusal, printed and dropped.

import sh.sysl.ogol.*
import sysl.io.{stdin, console_lines}
import sysl.term.edit.editor
import sysl.term.tty.{raw, cooked, tty_writer}

// The whole session, over whatever is handing it lines.
//
// **`*Iterate[string]` is what makes one loop serve every console.** An `Editor` and a `Lines` are
// both one, so the platform decides which to build and nothing below this line knows the difference —
// which is the whole of what keeps the desktop console and the board's from drifting apart.
session(cursor: *Iterate[string])
    var interp = ogol()

    // What has been typed and not yet run, because it was not finished. Empty is the ordinary state.
    var pending = ""

    print("Ogol. Type a line, or 'bye' to leave.")
    prints("ogol> ")

    for line in cursor
        if pending == "" && line == "bye" then break

        var src = if pending == "" then line else pending + "\n" + line

        pending = ""

        run(interp, src) match
            // A command answers with nothing, and a console that echoed a blank line for every
            // `print` would double every program's output.
            Ok(v) ->
                v match
                    Nothing -> ()
                    _ -> print(render(v))
            Err(f) ->
                f match
                    More -> pending = src
                    Bad(message) -> print(message)

        prints(if pending == "" then "ogol> " else ".... ")

    print("")
end session

// Which console this is, which is the only platform-dependent code here.
//
// **`raw` failing is not an error — it is the other situation.** At a terminal it puts the kernel out
// of the way so the editor can see a keystroke as it is typed; with input redirected from a file or a
// pipe there is no terminal to change, and then the editor is the wrong facility entirely: nothing is
// being typed, nothing should be echoed, and `console_lines` is what reads a stream of lines.
//
// So `ogol-repl < script.ogol` still works, and works *better* than it would through an editor.
var input = stdin()

if raw()
    // `tty_writer` and not `stdout`, because what the editor echoes has to appear as it is typed
    // rather than when a newline eventually flushes the buffer.
    var ed = editor(&input, tty_writer())

    session(&ed)
    cooked()
else
    var cursor = console_lines(&input)

    session(&cursor)
